<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hezb-e Wahdat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hizb-i Wahdat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hezb-e Wahdat is an Afghan political party founded in 1989 as a union of Shia factions representing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a> people, under Abdul Ali Mazari. During the civil war of the 1990s it fought other factions for control of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. It later joined the Northern Alliance against the Taliban, which killed Mazari in 1995.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hezb-e-wahdat.jpg" alt="Hezb-e Wahdat" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MrPenguin20 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_Hezbe_Wahdat.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the pro-Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> government collapsed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazarajat">Hazarajat</a> in 1979, the region passed to the Shura-ye Inqilab and then to rival radical Islamist groups, none able to control it. By the later 1980s each held its own pocket of territory. The Shura-ye Etelaf, an alliance of eight Shia organisations formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> in 1985, gave them a common voice towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> parties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, but these parties left the Tehran alliance out of their talks on an interim government after the Soviet withdrawal was announced in January 1988.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a> mujahideen took Bamyan in August 1988, and the town hosted the meeting that declared the Misaq-e Wahdat, the unity treaty, in July 1989. Unlike earlier coalitions it was made inside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazarajat">Hazarajat</a> rather than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, whose officials feared losing their hold on the movement but came to support the new party. Nasr and Pasdaran, the two main groups, won proportional representation, and smaller ones were pressed to join. Shaikh Asif Mohsini's Harakat Islami, dominated by non-Hazara Shiites, stayed out, and in Jaghori the Nahzat commander Wasiq was defeated in 1993.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A Supreme Supervisory Council of religious figures stood above a Central Council, the party's real deciding body. Planned at 36 members, it grew to more than 80 after the first congress of September 1991, and sharing power in it held the once hostile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a> groups together. Provincial and district councils reported to headquarters in Bamyan. Most of its leaders held political Shia Islamism, but the party drew steadily closer to its Hazara base.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the Second Afghan Civil War it was one of the main forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. It suffered three great defeats: its fall in Kabul and the killing of its leader Mazari by the Taliban in March 1995; the fall of Mazar-e Sharif in August 1998, when thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazaras</a> were massacred or imprisoned; and the loss of Bamyan weeks later. Its cadres fled abroad. After 2001 it entered the new administration with little weight; its last major force, the Ninth Corps, was disbanded in June 2005, and rivalry between Karim Khalili and Muhammad Mohaqiq split it into at least four parties claiming its name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hezbe Wahdat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gulbuddin Hekmatyar's Hezb-e Islami, Abdul Rashid Dostum's Uzbek militia and the Hazara <b>Hezb-e Wahdat</b> fought each other and the government for Kabul, killing tens of thousands of civilians in shelling.</blockquote><a href="/state/islamic-state-of-afghanistan">Islamic State of Afghanistan</a></li></ul></section>
